Kept the traversal to one pass with three pointers. `slow` moves one node, `fast` moves two, and `previous` stays just behind `slow`. When `fast` reaches the end, `slow` is the middle node, so `previous->next` can skip it.

The list is modified directly. The one-node case returns `nullptr`, and the two-node case removes the second node correctly.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteMiddle(ListNode* head) {
        if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        ListNode* previous = nullptr;
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {
            previous = slow;
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        }

        previous->next = slow->next;
        return head;
    }
};
```

Dry run on `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4`: after the first move, `previous` is 1, `slow` is 2, and `fast` is 3. The next fast move reaches the end, so node 3 is selected and removed.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Counting the nodes first makes the middle position easy to find. The middle index is `length / 2`, so after the count pass, the second walk stops at the node just before it and skips the middle node.

The empty and one-node cases return `null`. This changes the links in the input list, and Java can later clean up the removed node.

```java
/* Definition of singly-linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
 */

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteMiddle(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }

        int length = 0;
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            length++;
            current = current.next;
        }

        int middleIndex = length / 2;
        current = head;

        for (int i = 1; i < middleIndex; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        current.next = current.next.next;
        return head;
    }
}
```

For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5`, the length is 5 and the middle index is 2. `current` stops at node 2, then node 3 is skipped.

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the list is scanned twice.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version uses recursion to move through the list. At each call, `slow` moves one step and `fast` moves two steps. When `fast` reaches the end, `slow` is the middle node and `previous` can unlink it.

The code uses the call stack, so it needs more space than an iterative pointer solution. It still changes the existing list instead of creating new nodes.

```go
/*
Definition for singly-linked list.
type ListNode struct {
    Val  int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func deleteMiddle(head *ListNode) *ListNode {
    if head == nil || head.Next == nil {
        return nil
    }

    var remove func(previous, slow, fast *ListNode)
    remove = func(previous, slow, fast *ListNode) {
        if fast == nil || fast.Next == nil {
            previous.Next = slow.Next
            return
        }

        remove(slow, slow.Next, fast.Next.Next)
    }

    remove(nil, head, head)
    return head
}
```

An empty list and a single-node list return immediately. For two nodes, the recursive call reaches `fast == nil` with `previous` at the first node and `slow` at the second, so the second node is removed.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursive calls.

Starting `fast` two nodes ahead lets `slow` stop one node before the middle. That means no separate `previous` pointer is needed. The middle node is removed with `slow->next = slow->next->next`.

This handles even and odd list sizes with the required middle choice. The input links are changed directly, and the one-node list becomes empty.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteMiddle(ListNode* head) {
        if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head->next->next;

        while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        }

        slow->next = slow->next->next;
        return head;
    }
};
```

For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5`, `fast` starts at 3. After one loop it reaches 5 and `slow` reaches 2. The next node, 3, is the middle and gets skipped.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.
